<p>European Union foreign ministers failed to finalize a new round of sanctions on Russia and could not green-light an emergency loan of about €90 billion for Ukraine after Hungary withheld its consent, with Slovakia also opposing the sanctions package. The setback came as EU leaders prepared to travel to Kyiv on Tuesday for commemoration events marking four years since Russia’s full-scale invasion, leaving the visit with fewer concrete deliverables than planned. Hungary tied its stance to a dispute with Ukraine over oil supplies via the Soviet-era Druzhba pipeline, saying the disruption threatens its energy security. Ukraine has said Russia damaged the pipeline, which Moscow denies; Hungary has echoed that denial. EU officials and several member states said the veto undercuts unity and the bloc’s ability to keep supporting Ukraine, even as the EU moved ahead with narrower steps such as sanctioning specific Russian officials. Despite the deadlock, most EU countries are still pushing to keep pressure on Moscow and maintain financial support for Kyiv—signs of continued engagement, even if internal politics and energy concerns are slowing the pace.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/QNL4kcSfI73FVPQzVNVxPSVSl7FUEfaUz1GorIArV53HJq0c9wL3nq3TZjn4SOqvFVeLqMVMFrbrUyEqsB3ksI2AuaFo86huElfhOsfkDTbMcd2R5rMGrNKnXs9tbgyxznqDfz6gzGJ7U2oRhXcdMGahctfy' alt='EU leaders’ planned Kyiv visit was left without new sanctions or a fresh loan package after Hungary’s veto.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Energy-security argument: Hungary’s foreign minister Péter Szijjártó said Budapest would not accept what it described as energy-supply “blackmail” linked to the Druzhba pipeline dispute with Ukraine.</li><li>Kyiv response: Ukrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha told POLITICO that Hungary “demonizes Ukraine for political gain” and demanded Budapest stop using Ukraine in domestic politics.</li><li>EU visit impact: POLITICO reported that Commission President Ursula von der Leyen and Council President António Costa were due in Kyiv on Tuesday but, after the veto, could bring “little to offer other than condolences”.</li><li>Sanctions-package context: RFE/RL reported the EU wanted an agreement timed to the invasion anniversary; POLITICO described the stalled measures as the EU’s 20th sanctions package, showing how even a calendar-driven push can still run into unanimity hurdles.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Hungary (government): Budapest framed its stance around the Druzhba dispute, accusing Kyiv of deliberately withholding oil supplies and describing the situation as an “oil blockade,” while warning it would not accept “blackmail” over Hungarian energy security and echoing Russia’s denial of responsibility for the disruption. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/632935-hungary-ukraine-loan-veto/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Ukraine (foreign ministry): Kyiv accused Hungary’s leadership of using anti-Ukraine messaging for domestic political advantage and called on Budapest to stop linking Ukraine’s war needs to Hungary’s internal politics. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-ukraine-visit-hungary-blocks-russia-sanctions-funding-feud-viktor-orban/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li><li>Sweden (Europe minister): Sweden’s Europe Minister Jessica Rosencrantz described Hungary’s move as a “new low,” reflecting frustration among some member states with repeated veto threats. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-ukraine-visit-hungary-blocks-russia-sanctions-funding-feud-viktor-orban/'>POLITICO Europe</a>)</li><li>EU institutions (reporting/assessment): Al Jazeera reported that, alongside the failure to reach agreement on the broader new measures, the EU moved ahead with a narrower step by sanctioning specific Russian officials, underscoring how the bloc can still act in limited ways even when a full package stalls. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/23/world/europe/ukraine-war-russia-eu-hungary.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/23/world/europe/ukraine-war-russia-eu-hungary.html'>EU Support for Ukraine Stumbles as Hungary Looks to Delay Aid</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/hungary-slovakia-block-eu-sanctions-russia/33685525.html'>Hungary, Slovakia Block New EU Sanctions On Russia</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/632935-hungary-ukraine-loan-veto/'>Hungary vetoes €90 billion EU loan for Ukraine</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1rcv1xx/eu_accuses_hungary_of_disloyalty_for_vetoing_90/'>EU accuses Hungary of disloyalty for vetoing €90 billion loan to Ukraine</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/23/eu-sanctions-russian-officials-as-hungary-blocks-funds-to-ukraine'>EU sanctions Russian officials as Hungary blocks funds to Ukraine</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/hungary-vows-to-block-eu-sanctions-on-russia-on-eve-of-ukraine-war-anniversary'>Hungary blocks Russia sanctions, EU cash for Kyiv on eve of Ukraine war anniversary</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-ukraine-visit-hungary-blocks-russia-sanctions-funding-feud-viktor-orban/'>The EU is going to Ukraine empty-handed ― thanks to Hungary</a> - politico.eu</li></ul>